WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:10.940
You leap out of a flying bus And are now soaring through the strangely-purple skyline above

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:12.960
a cartoonish island.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:18.240
Your fingers clasped tightly around the handles of your hand-gilder — which is unironically

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.020
made of candy.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:24.710
You’re dressed head-to-toe in superhero-esque armour — eyes glowing.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:28.460
There’s also angels wings protruding from your back.

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:33.780
As the Tilted Towers rooftops get closer and you anticipate your first move, your head

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:38.489
swivels as you realize you’re surrounded by people who want to kill you!

00:00:38.489 --> 00:00:41.450
John Wick to to the left, Tomato Head to the
right.

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:45.859
You’re breaking into a building through the roof your excavation tool of choice is

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:53.280
an odd combination of a plunger fused with a katana — trying to find some way to stay alive.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:58.760
Your feet hit the floor and you quickly open a glimmering treasure chest in the hopes of

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:00.000
finding a weapon.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.320
Welcome to Fortnite.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:05.960
"I love fornite. I LOVE FORTNITE!"

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:13.920
Fortnite is taking the world by storm. But it didn’t happen overnight.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.120
The game was locked away in development for almost seven years after it was announced

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:19.960
back in 2011.

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:24.620
The developer, Epic Games was working on a brand new game engine called “Unreal

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:28.840
Engine 4” in order to build a game to rival Minecraft.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:36.040
"The community is incredible creativity has taken Minecraft into places we never could have imagined."

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:40.240
There are plenty of unforeseen difficulties associated with building a brand new game

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:45.119
engine, especially if you want it to rival one of the world’s most popular games.

00:01:45.119 --> 00:01:50.359
And that — along with Epic’s desire to make Fortnite feel like a living organism

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:54.920
with constant updates and additions — slowed down its development in a big way.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:57.580
Fortnite’s initial concept was simple.

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:02.479
You were going to get to build structures just like in Minecraft, but in Fortnite you’d

00:02:02.479 --> 00:02:07.979
be building a base to stave off monsters called “husks” at night and survive the storm

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:10.000
for two in-game weeks.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:15.100
It was going to be everything that was popular in 2011 squished together and blasted onto

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:17.420
your television screen from your console.

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:23.260
By 2014 Epic had a prototype version of the game ready for release and the Unreal Engine 4

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:28.400
was slowly being refined, but was held back to further smooth out the gameplay.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:33.480
The vision Epic had for this player versus environment iteration of the game is pretty

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.560
similar to the “Save the World” version we see now.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:42.520
"That kind of moment, that moment tha makes people cheer even if they're not playing is precisely what

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:43.600
Fortnite is about."

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:50.400
By June 2017, Fortnite was finally ready for the public, but the hype had died down significantly

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:53.460
since its initial announcement six years ago.

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:58.320
It was crisp, colorful, and combined the building assets of Minecraft with a shooter

00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:01.879
like Halo or Gears of War — just like Epic wanted.

00:03:01.879 --> 00:03:05.920
Guns, four-by-four structures and zombies…it had it all.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:10.020
But the game more or less tripped on its way out of Tomato Town.

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:14.260
What was taking off though, was a different game format that players just couldn’t get

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:16.520
enough of — Battle Royale.

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:29.520
Battle Royale games were taking the gaming world by storm, but they began mostly as Minecraft

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:35.460
mods that were stylized around pop culture storylines like the Hunger Games by fans of both genres.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:41.260
The format is simple: Contestants are all trapped in a remote location and must fight

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:45.760
one another by any means necessary until only one winner is left standing.

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:50.040
"And may the odds be ever in your favor."

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:56.020
The first non-mod Battle Royale game that had significant success was H1Z1.

00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:01.140
This was the first Battle Royale brainchild from Brendan “PlayerUnknown” Green,

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:06.440
an Irish photographer and web designer turned battle royale developer, who had been working

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:08.030
on mods for a while.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:12.880
He would then go on to create his own project — PlayerUnknown’s Battlegrounds.

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:16.480
"And then last year, Bluehole contacted me from Korea.

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:20.560
And said, "We love what you've done with the battle royale game. We'd like you to lead a team and

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.440
make your own battle royale. And PUBG was born out of that."

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:29.800
PUBG launched in early access during March of 2017 and it’s hard to overstate just

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:31.780
how groundbreaking it was.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:37.100
Funnily enough, the game's developer Bluehole utilized Epic's Unreal Engine 4

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:41.680
in order to ensure the game could handle the studio's new 100 person bloodbath.,

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:46.460
Yes that same engine being used to build Fortnite.

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:53.760
PUBG averaged over 100,000 viewers at a time in both August and September of 2017 and even

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:56.900
attracted esports attention from the likes of ESL.

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:09.200
(Casting)

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:15.560
Battle Royale was in, and with 8 million copies sold that August and 10 million that September,

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:18.000
people couldn’t get enough of it.

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:27.080
(Stream)

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:33.980
So after an eternity in development Fortnite made its debut in the summer of 2017 as a bridesmaid,

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:38.460
while the bell of the ball, PUBG and Battle Royales had all eyes on it.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:45.300
On September 26, 2017 Epic Games made a move to keep up with PUBG.

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:48.860
They simply put the world’s biggest game, into their game.

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:53.820
They gave Fortnite the biggest shot in the arm you could give a game in 2017

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:57.200
They added a Battle Royale Mode of their own.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:03.660
PUBG may have been the first battle royale game to take off, but when Epic Games dropped

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:09.360
Fortnite’s Battle Royale mode, it became clear that the genre had yet to hit its ceiling.

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:14.760
Not only did Fortnite beat PUBG to console, but its Battle Royale mode was free to play

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:17.660
while PUBG’s cost 30$.

00:06:17.660 --> 00:06:24.800
And unlike PUBG, Fortnite offered an irreverent, playful-style that appealed to gamers of all ages.

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:33.660
Battle Royale is inspired by an undoubtedly gruesome narrative.

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:38.640
But, Fortnite works hard to juxtapose the grim fate of that island with the kind of

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:44.060
bouncy, bubbly and bombastic gameplay you’d expect from a Disney Pixar movie.

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:48.120
After all, what could be more fun than wearing your bunny costume into battle.

00:06:48.260 --> 00:06:54.740
(Stream)

00:06:54.740 --> 00:07:00.920
According to Epic Games, Fortnite broke PUBG’s concurrent players record in February 2018,

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:03.860
hitting 3.4 million concurrents.

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:08.400
"And Amazon's Twitch saw its March traffic surge 40% thanks to Fortnite.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:10.660
The most watched game in Twitch history."

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:16.900
The Battle Royale bubble has burst — and Fortnite has been crowned master of the genre,

00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:18.980
at least for now.

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:31.960
Between April 22 and May 22 of 2018 Twitch viewers watched 139,333,216 hours of Fortnite.

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:36.040
And the game has quite literally become  a cultural phenomenon.

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:36.800
"What have you been playing?"

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:37.400
"Fortnite."

00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:39.380
"Yeh."

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:41.480
"Did you get season four already?"

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:42.300
"Nah I didn't."

00:07:42.340 --> 00:07:43.780
"Dang it you gotta download it."

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:44.480
"It's tight."

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:51.700
So needless to say, the relationship between Epic Games and PUBG developers Bluehole has

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:56.169
become a little awkward since Epic has now taken their Unreal Engine and built their

00:07:56.169 --> 00:07:58.360
own Battle Royale game around it.

00:07:58.360 --> 00:08:05.680
As a result, on May 29 PUBG filed a copyright violation lawsuit asking the court to decide

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:09.159
whether Fortnite was a copy of PUBG.

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:15.700
But, despite this looming lawsuit, Fortnite continues to leap into the mainstream like the

00:08:15.700 --> 00:08:17.280
game just ate a hop-rock.

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:22.860
"It's all over social media, pretty much all my friends. You can literally walk on the street and see someone

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:25.720
playing on their phone. And everyone is talking about it."

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:28.860
"I know the game, I've heard references to it online.

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:31.200
People getting into trouple playing it too much.

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:32.640
Girlfriends and boyfriends you know."

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:37.280
"I've played once. And I got number 25 so I'm pretty proud of that. That's it."

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:42.220
"And I have this teacher who plays it and talks about it with my classmates.

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:47.400
When they play it and they talk about it over class. It's kind of annoying."

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:51.820
In game, what's most different about Fortnite

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:53.080
is its pace.

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:58.620
"It's a game, obviously number one: optimized."

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:02.540
"And that is why its popularity is just like *jet noises* a rocket." "I should try it."

00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:03.620
"Yeah you'll love it."

00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:09.460
When you die, you can get back in the queue within seconds and be right back in the action.

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:11.900
It offers normal gun fights.

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:17.040
Building fights that turn into editing fights.

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:22.600
(Stream)

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:25.260
And jetpack fights among others.

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:29.240
(Stream)

00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:34.480
The only thing more limitless than the fun, is the capacity for skillful gameplay.

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:40.880
(Stream)

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:46.200
After its Battle Royale mode was released, it took Fortnite just three short months to

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:50.360
show comparable average Twitch viewership numbers to PUBG.

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:57.940
By February it had completely eclipsed its predecessor with an average of 97,904 viewers

00:09:57.940 --> 00:10:02.200
to PUBG’s 64,654.

00:10:02.300 --> 00:10:09.280
As of May, Fortnite has taken over Twitch and the game is averaging more than 178,000

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:14.400
viewers at any given time, completely dismantling its competition.

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:19.340
"You jump in, you get to experience the whole story for an episode, start to finish because it's a game.

00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:21.100
And you get to feel like you're a part of something."

00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:24.460
"I think that is massive for viewership."

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:27.240
"This is the League of Legends killer."

00:10:28.300 --> 00:10:30.280
So what was the breaking point?

00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:36.400
Well — it came in the form of a 26-year-old former Halo pro turned streamer.

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:46.200
On March 15 Tyler “Ninja” Blevins massacred the Twitch individual streaming record when

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:52.140
he played a Fortnite squads match with rappers Drake and Travis Scott and Pittsburgh Steelers

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:54.920
wide receiver Juju Smith-Schuster.

00:10:55.200 --> 00:11:01.200
(Stream)

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:06.580
As soon as “The Boy Duddus” touched down on Fortnite island — the barrier to entry

00:11:06.580 --> 00:11:11.820
to video games and streaming fell, and Fortnite became a household name.

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:19.080
"I think that that literal one match with Drake made it okay for everyone to play video games."

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:24.720
It’s not like gaming wasn’t big before — but in that moment — Fortnite became

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:28.480
mainstream  in a way that few games have.

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:30.240
"Fortnite is free to play."

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:34.640
"And accessible across consoles, PCs, and iOS mobile devices."

00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:37.920
"It's the online game that has fans in a frenzy."

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:44.140
"Yeah I didn't start playing until Drake started playing so you can blame him on this one."

00:11:44.260 --> 00:11:52.000
That night Ninja broke the Twitch viewership record with more than 630,000 concurrent viewers.

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:58.120
Just a few weeks later late in April, he broke his own record with Ninja Vegas,

00:11:58.120 --> 00:12:00.080
his very own esports event.

00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:07.660
(Casting)

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:12.560
The event peaked at around 679,000 concurrents.

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:17.320
"I can't even imagine what that looks like if you saw that many people in person."

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:18.660
"Terrifying, most likely."

00:12:18.760 --> 00:12:25.380
After these two massive streaming splashes, Ninja’s stream took over the Twitch world.

00:12:25.520 --> 00:12:33.660
As of May 23, 2018 Ninja was averaging more than 125,000 viewers every day while he streamed.

00:12:33.980 --> 00:12:41.440
He finished April more popular on social media than the likes of Cristiano Ronaldo and Shaquille O’Neal.

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:48.720
Fortnite was vaulted into the living room-small-talk zeitgeist — and Ninja was right there with it.

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:53.620
So in a world of this:

00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:56.400
This:

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:00.880
And this:

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:06.840
"Red Sox pitcher David Price said Thursday that video games are not the cause of his carpal tunnel

00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:12.000
syndrome, but stressed he will not play the popular game Fortnite in the team's clubhouse again quote:

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:15.320
"to avoid the distraction it has already caused" end quote.

00:13:15.320 --> 00:13:17.360
What's the next step for Fortnite?

00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:21.680
Well, in a traditional sense it’s been esports.

00:13:23.340 --> 00:13:28.340
Previous games that have splashed into relevancy have tried to strengthen their foothold on

00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:33.260
the gaming community by branching their programming into competitive events.

00:13:33.260 --> 00:13:39.580
Major esports organizations like Team SoloMid and FaZe Clan have already made big commitments

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:45.600
by signing players and building Fornite teams in a reaction to the game’s massive popularity.

00:13:45.760 --> 00:13:55.580
(Stream)

00:13:55.580 --> 00:14:02.580
In theory this incentivizes players to log hours and compete to make a career out of the game.

00:14:02.860 --> 00:14:05.400
But Fortnite’s a little different.

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:11.100
The games aren’t the long, team-focused slogs that you’ll find in League of Legends or Dota 2.

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:16.900
They’re snappy and quick and it’s not like you can hold a camera over the top of the entire island.

00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:24.320
Not to mention the logistical issues associated with putting 100 rigs and professionals in a room together.

00:14:24.360 --> 00:14:30.120
But Fortnite and Epic Games are making a run at making esports a possibility.

00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:37.560
On May 21, Epic announced that they would be putting up $100 million towards Fortnite

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:41.120
esports between 2018 and 2019.

00:14:41.200 --> 00:14:52.960
(Stream)

00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:59.280
Since then, during the Fortnite Pro-Am at E3, Epic also announced where that money would

00:14:59.280 --> 00:15:02.640
be going — the Fortnite World Cup.

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:08.420
"Qualifying for the Fortnite world cup will be based on merit, and Epic will not be selling teams or franchises

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:11.060
and we won't allow third party leagues to do so either.

00:15:11.140 --> 00:15:17.060
This event will spread that $100 million over a number of different online and LAN qualifying

00:15:17.060 --> 00:15:19.360
events all over the world.

00:15:19.360 --> 00:15:25.240
This will all culminate in the World Cup, for which details have not yet been announced for.

00:15:25.300 --> 00:15:30.540
Before Fortnite even has a competitive game mode, they’re willing to come out and say

00:15:30.540 --> 00:15:32.740
Fortnite is here to stay.

00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:37.520
Just a day later, Epic announced Fortnite’s involvement in E3.

00:15:37.520 --> 00:15:42.700
Where they hosted a $3,000,000 charity
pro-am at the gaming showcase featuring the

00:15:42.700 --> 00:15:48.840
likes of: Ninja, Myth, Marshmallow and NBA star Paul George.

00:15:49.020 --> 00:15:53.620
Ninja and Marshmallow were crowned champions at the Fortnite Pro-Am.

00:15:53.620 --> 00:16:01.200
And as he raised his golden pickaxe with a peak viewership of 1.5 million watching online,

00:16:01.200 --> 00:16:05.200
Ninja’s stranglehold as the “king of fortnite” tightened.

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:15.640
So Fortnite has built itself into this towering structure of commercial success and absolute fan obsession.

00:16:15.860 --> 00:16:20.760
There are enemies on all sides, competing games and critics armed with boogey bombs

00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:22.340
and pump shotguns.

00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:27.120
There are in-game references showing up all over popular culture.

00:16:31.640 --> 00:16:36.720
Your parents know what Fortnite is, your teacher spends their night chasing Victory Royales

00:16:36.840 --> 00:16:43.140
and your social media feeds are flooded with highlights, memes and Fortnite players busting a move.

00:16:47.240 --> 00:16:51.660
It wasn’t that long ago that we thought PUBG was the next big thing.

00:16:51.660 --> 00:16:56.820
That the rest of our gaming lives would be spent on that post-apocalyptic wasteland.

00:16:56.820 --> 00:17:00.440
Will Fortnite become the understudy just as quickly?

00:17:00.680 --> 00:17:03.960
Or are we bound to that island?

00:17:03.960 --> 00:17:09.640
Building ramps up to the sky, riding rockets to get that jaw-dropping kill and leaping

00:17:09.640 --> 00:17:16.080
off that launch pad to avoid the storm and fly into Fortnite’s limitless future.

